    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>I make the strongest defensible claim: not that Einstein was a fraud who stole the work, but that he was a tactically skilled outsider who used the formal tools of the patent office—novelty, self-contained claims, precise language—to reclassify a collaborative, multinational research front into a single-author breakthrough. The citation record shows the tactical effect. Whether the intent was conscious or the product of genuine belief in the novelty of his conceptual frame is a separate question the historical record cannot settle. What the record can settle is that the semantic architecture of the 1905 papers performed the exclusion, and the subsequent hagiography made the performance permanent (see the popular and biographical literature surveyed in Isaacson 2007, and the critical historiography in Galison 2003 and Stachel 1998).</p>

<h3 class="section">🕳️ Operation Five: The Placeholder Inherited as Territory</h3>

<p>The deepest operation concerns "spacetime curvature." Einstein treated curvature as a mathematical representation—a geometric analogy for the metric structure, chosen because the mathematics of Riemannian geometry fit the physical intuition of tidal gravity. The analogy was declared. In inheritance, the declaration was dropped. "Spacetime curvature" is now taught as though it names a feature of territory rather than a cartographic choice.</p>

<p>The cosmological constant is the paradigm of the placeholder logic. Einstein introduced Λ in 1917 by stipulation to produce a static universe; it was withdrawn when Hubble showed expansion; it was restored in 1998 by stipulation to accommodate accelerated expansion. Its value is measured, not derived; its interpretation is unresolved. Contemporary philosophy of cosmology explicitly describes the standard cosmological model as consisting of "placeholders that represent the 'simplest ideas that would allow a fit to the observations'." This is E07 (PLACEHOLDER_AS_REFERENT) and E03 operating at the level of the inherited vocabulary.</p>

